msgid "A note on QPU time {#a-note-on-qpu-time}"
msgstr "Tungkol sa QPU time {#a-note-on-qpu-time}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The QPU time a function run needs depends strongly on the chosen options, the number of warm-start iterations, and shots. With the default options, the function needs considerably less QPU time than standard QAOA, because it uses iterative warm-starting instead of a variational parameter training loop on the QPU.\n"
msgstr "Malaki ang epekto ng mga napiling option, ng bilang ng warm-start iteration, at ng mga shot sa QPU time na kailangan ng isang function run. Sa mga default option, mas kaunti nang malaki ang QPU time na kailangan ng function kaysa sa standard QAOA, dahil gumagamit ito ng iterative warm-starting sa halip na variational parameter training loop sa QPU.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As an example, both approaches were compared  on `ibm_phoenix` using Maximum Independent Set instances that correspond to function sizes `M` and `L`. A [standard QAOA workflow](/tutorials/quantum-approximate-optimization-algorithm) needed about 130 and 200 seconds of QPU time, respectively. The function with default options needed about 30 seconds in both cases. Results for other problems and settings will differ.\n"
msgstr "Bilang halimbawa, pinaghambing ang dalawang paraan sa `ibm_phoenix` gamit ang mga Maximum Independent Set instance na tumutugma sa function size na `M` at `L`. Ang isang [standard QAOA workflow](/tutorials/quantum-approximate-optimization-algorithm) ay nangailangan ng humigit-kumulang 130 at 200 segundo ng QPU time, ayon sa pagkakasunod. Ang function na may default option ay nangailangan ng humigit-kumulang 30 segundo sa parehong kaso. Iba ang magiging resulta para sa ibang problema at setting.\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-09-30 · verified 2026-10-01
